# A draft plan is never committed by accident: `git add -A` during
# implementation would otherwise stage it, and a plan is exactly the document
# that might name an unpatched vulnerability, an auth-bypass specific, a
# customer or an embargoed launch.
#
# The APPROVED plan is committed deliberately, with `git add -f`, once the
# disclosure check passes, and merges with the first increment's PR
# (claude-core.md Planning rule 3; plan-review-loop, Close-out item 4). Once
# tracked, this pattern no longer applies to it, so amendments stage normally.
#
# COMMITTED RATHER THAN LEFT TO THE SCRIPT. In THIS repository the directory is
# used by hand -- a plan copied in before any exchange has run is a plan nothing
# is ignoring yet, which is a gap reached during the loop's own live validation
# on 2026-09-18. Committing it makes the protection exist before the script
# rather than because of it. The pattern matches what the script looks for, so a
# later run finds it and appends nothing.
#
# THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM the script "protects a fresh consumer", which was
# false and is the reason the gap stayed invisible: a scope exchange is a
# planning exchange and wrote nothing, so a consumer's draft sat unignored
# between the scope exchange and the first assessment. What protects a consumer
# is `core/docs/plans/.gitignore`, which the sync delivers; this file is this
# repository's own copy and `scripts/check-root-wiring.mjs` holds the two
# copies' pattern lines identical (#124).
PLAN_*.md
